  "summary": "Unsupervised and self-supervised methods now target spillover, batch effects and background noise in cytometry and multiplexed imaging data.",
  "synthesis": "The four items converge on computational correction of technical artefacts in high-dimensional cytometry and multiplexed imaging, namely spillover, batch effects, background noise and quality control. Elflow applies a modular self-supervised VICReg framework with automated QC and is benchmarked on public flow and CyTOF datasets. SpillOT addresses spillover in highly multiplexed images with a zero-shot, unsupervised optimal-transport denoiser. BioBatchNet uses a dual-encoder adversarial design to separate batch from biological signal, enabling controllable correction in imaging mass cytometry. mapfx.norm removes background noise and batch effects from massively parallel cytometry data. What is new is the emphasis on unsupervised and self-supervised learning: SpillOT is zero-shot and unsupervised, and Elflow relies on self-supervised representations. Elflow is a preprint, while the other three are peer-reviewed publications.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
